From: Codex folio backport <codex@local>
Subject: [PATCH] E404 folio G2.5c: Convert mapping_get_entry to return a folio

Upstream patch 87/90.  The page cache only ever holds folios, so the entry
loaded from the xarray already is the head, and the speculation can go
straight on it instead of on a head obtained with compound_head().

The second half of the patch follows from the first: because the object
we hold a reference on and the object we compare against xas_reload()
are now the same one, E404's separate "the page was split under us"
re-check is gone.  Upstream reaches the same place from the other
direction, by deleting the equivalent check in mapping_get_entry().

Divergences from upstream, all deliberate:

  - E404 called this function find_get_entry() and returned struct page *.
    Upstream calls it mapping_get_entry() and returns void *, so that one
    type tells the caller whether it got a folio or a shadow/swap entry.
    Both names are kept here: mapping_get_entry() is the converted static
    function, and find_get_entry() becomes a thin exported wrapper that
    hands back the head page for callers that have not been converted yet
    (two in mm/filemap.c, one in mm/memcontrol.c).  Upstream converts
    those callers in patches 88/90 and 89/90, after which the wrapper goes
    away.

  - The wrapper must not lose the shadow/swap case, so it checks both
    NULL and xa_is_value() before dereferencing.  A shadow entry has its
    low bit set, and a NULL entry is not a folio, so a bare
    page_folio() would be nonsense for either.

  - page_folio() is a _Generic macro whose two arms are struct page * and
    const struct page *, so the wrapper casts explicitly before calling it.
    A void * controlling expression would select no arm and fail to build.

folio_try_get_rcu() and E404's page_cache_get_speculative() are the same
operation here: with CONFIG_TINY_RCU unset (this build has
CONFIG_PREEMPT_RCU=y and no TINY_RCU) the first is
folio_ref_add_unless(folio, 1, 0) and the second is
get_page_unless_zero().  The only thing dropped is page_cache_get_speculative()'s
VM_BUG_ON_PAGE(PageTail(page)), which a folio satisfies by construction.

diff --git a/mm/filemap.c b/mm/filemap.c
index 43b3d5f..64259a1 100644
--- a/mm/filemap.c
+++ b/mm/filemap.c
@@ -1834,59 +1834,80 @@ pgoff_t page_cache_prev_miss(struct address_space *mapping,
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(page_cache_prev_miss);
 
 /**
- * find_get_entry - find and get a page cache entry
+ * mapping_get_entry - Look up the page cache entry
  * @mapping: the address_space to search
- * @offset: the page cache index
+ * @index: The page cache index.
  *
- * Looks up the page cache slot at @mapping & @offset.  If there is a
- * page cache page, it is returned with an increased refcount.
+ * Looks up the page cache entry at @mapping & @index.  If it is a folio,
+ * it is returned with an increased refcount.  If it is a shadow entry
+ * of a previously evicted folio, or a swap entry from shmem/tmpfs,
+ * it is returned without further action.
  *
- * If the slot holds a shadow entry of a previously evicted page, or a
- * swap entry from shmem/tmpfs, it is returned.
+ * Return: The folio, swap or shadow entry, %NULL if nothing is found.
  *
- * Return: the found page or shadow entry, %NULL if nothing is found.
+ * Upstream patch 87/90.  E404 called this find_get_entry() and returned
+ * struct page *.  It speculated on the head page obtained with
+ * compound_head(), then re-checked that the page had not been split
+ * underneath.  The page cache only ever holds folios, so the entry loaded
+ * from the xarray already is the head: the speculation goes straight on it
+ * with folio_try_get_rcu(), and the object we hold a reference on and the
+ * object we compare against xas_reload() become the same one, which is what
+ * makes the separate split check unnecessary.
  */
-struct page *find_get_entry(struct address_space *mapping, pgoff_t offset)
+static void *mapping_get_entry(struct address_space *mapping, pgoff_t index)
 {
-	XA_STATE(xas, &mapping->i_pages, offset);
-	struct page *head, *page;
+	XA_STATE(xas, &mapping->i_pages, index);
+	struct folio *folio;
 
 	rcu_read_lock();
 repeat:
 	xas_reset(&xas);
-	page = xas_load(&xas);
-	if (xas_retry(&xas, page))
+	folio = xas_load(&xas);
+	if (xas_retry(&xas, folio))
 		goto repeat;
 	/*
 	 * A shadow entry of a recently evicted page, or a swap entry from
 	 * shmem/tmpfs.  Return it without attempting to raise page count.
 	 */
-	if (!page || xa_is_value(page))
+	if (!folio || xa_is_value(folio))
 		goto out;
 
-	head = compound_head(page);
-	if (!page_cache_get_speculative(head))
+	if (!folio_try_get_rcu(folio))
 		goto repeat;
 
-	/* The page was split under us? */
-	if (compound_head(page) != head) {
-		put_page(head);
-		goto repeat;
-	}
-
-	/*
-	 * Has the page moved?
-	 * This is part of the lockless pagecache protocol. See
-	 * include/linux/pagemap.h for details.
-	 */
-	if (unlikely(page != xas_reload(&xas))) {
-		put_page(head);
+	if (unlikely(folio != xas_reload(&xas))) {
+		folio_put(folio);
 		goto repeat;
 	}
 out:
 	rcu_read_unlock();
 
-	return page;
+	return folio;
+}
+
+/**
+ * find_get_entry - find and get a page cache entry
+ * @mapping: the address_space to search
+ * @offset: the page cache index
+ *
+ * Compatibility wrapper for callers that have not been converted to folios;
+ * upstream converts them in patches 88/90 and 89/90.  The page cache only
+ * holds folios, so the head page is what the entry would have been anyway.
+ *
+ * If the slot holds a shadow entry of a previously evicted page, or a
+ * swap entry from shmem/tmpfs, it is returned unchanged and the caller must
+ * use xa_is_value() to tell.
+ *
+ * Return: the found page or shadow entry, %NULL if nothing is found.
+ */
+struct page *find_get_entry(struct address_space *mapping, pgoff_t offset)
+{
+	void *entry = mapping_get_entry(mapping, offset);
+
+	if (!entry || xa_is_value(entry))
+		return (struct page *)entry;
+
+	return &page_folio((struct page *)entry)->page;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(find_get_entry);
 
